## Evidence and disposition

Java `MessageQueueAssignment.equals` compares the queue only. Its `hashCode`
also includes `MessageRequestMode.hashCode()` and attachments. Since the enum
does not override `Object.hashCode`, the mode hash is process-local. The invoked
fixture constructs an attachment whose hash cancels the mode-hash difference
for that JVM; both assignments compare equal, have equal hash values, and the
Java `HashSet` retains one. Ordinary same-queue/different-mode assignments
with different hashes remain distinct.

Rust's `java_equals` maps the public Java method. Rust `Eq`/`Hash` remains
lawful and compares all fields. A Java Set serialized after insertion already
contains the membership result chosen by that JVM. A raw JSON collection
engineered to collide for one JVM cannot have stable cross-process semantics,
so this local collection behavior is recorded as an implementation boundary,
not a portable wire-flow mismatch. Compatibility and known-difference records
now state the exact boundary.

